==Central Franconian==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|gmw-cfr|/də/}}

===Article===
{{head|gmw-cfr|articles|definite, reduced}}

# [[the]]
## {{lb|gmw-cfr|most dialects}} {{n-g|[[feminine]] [[nominative]] and [[accusative]]}}
## {{lb|gmw-cfr|most dialects}} {{n-g|[[plural]] nominative and accusative}}
## {{lb|gmw-cfr|many dialects}} {{n-g|plural [[dative]]}}
## {{lb|gmw-cfr|some dialects}} {{n-g|[[masculine]] nominative}}
## {{lb|gmw-cfr|some dialects}} {{n-g|masculine accusative}}
## {{lb|gmw-cfr|few dialects}} {{n-g|feminine dative}}

====Usage notes====
* {{sense|masculine}} Three territories must be distinguished: 1.) [[Ripuarian]], in which the accusative takes the form of the nominative; 2.) western [[Moselle Franconian]], in which the nominative takes the form of the accusative; 3.) eastern Moselle Franconian, in which nominative and accusative are distinct.
:: 1.) In Ripuarian, the reduced masculine article in nominative and accusative is '''''de''''' only in a few places, including [[w:Bonn|Bonn]]; most dialects have {{m|gmw-cfr|der}}. The full form is always {{m|gmw-cfr|dä}}.
:: 2.) In western Moselle Franconian, the form is '''''de''''', but becomes {{m|gmw-cfr|den}} before vowels, ''h-'', and dental consonants. The full form is {{m|gmw-cfr|dän}}.
:: 3.) In eastern Moselle Franconian, the reduced masculine article in the nominative is '''''de''''' in many dialects, ''der'' in others. The full form is {{m|gmw-cfr|där}}. The accusative takes ''den'' (full form: ''dän'').
* {{sense|feminine}} Virtually all dialects use '''''de''''' as the reduced feminine article in nominative and accusative. The full form is {{m|gmw-cfr|die}}. In the dative, '''''de''''' is used in a few dialects of Ripuarian; the general form is ''der''. The full form may be {{m|gmw-cfr|där}} or ''dä''.
* {{sense|plural}} Virtually all dialects use '''''de''''' as the reduced plural article in nominative and accusative. The full form is ''die''. In the dative, '''''de''''' is used in most dialects of Ripuarian. In Moselle Franconian the form is the same as the masculine accusative (see above). The full form of the dative plural may be ''dä, dän,'' or {{m|gmw-cfr|däne}}.
* Westernmost Ripuarian has no case distinction whatsoever. Only the nominative forms are relevant for these dialects.

