====Article====
{{head|fr|articles|indefinite}}

# {{n-g|Used in the plural with prepositioned adjectives.}}
#: {{ux|inline=1|fr|Ce sont '''de''' bons enfants.|They are good children.}}
#: {{ux|inline=1|fr|Il y a '''d’'''autres exemples.|There are other examples.}}
# {{n-g|Used in negated sentences with the grammatical object.}}
#: {{ux|inline=1|fr|Elle n’a pas '''de''' mère.|She doesn’t have a mother.}}
#: {{ux|inline=1|fr|Il ne mange pas '''de''' viande.|He doesn’t eat meat.}}
#: {{ux|inline=1|fr|Il n’y a pas '''de''' problèmes.|There are no problems.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In negative sentences, '''{{m|fr||de}}''' often replaces the indefinite ({{m|fr|un}}, {{m|fr|une}} and {{m|fr|des}}) and partitive articles ({{m|fr|du}}, {{m|fr|de la}}, {{m|fr|des}}). However, there are situations where the indefinite or partitive articles are retained. For example<ref>[https://bdl.oqlf.gouv.qc.ca/bdl/gabarit_bdl.asp?Th=2&t1=&id=3514 Banque de dépannage linguistique]</ref>:
** when the nominal element is an attributive complement to the negated verb {{m|fr|être}}
**: {{ux|inline=1|fr|Il n'est pas un menteur.|He isn't a liar.}}
** when the complement of the negated verb is followed by a contradistinctive element (not X, but Y)
**: {{ux|inline=1|fr|Il ne mange pas de viande.|He doesn't eat meat.}}
**: {{ux|inline=1|fr|Il ne mange pas de la viande, mais du pain.|He doesn't eat meat, but bread.}}

