====Usage notes====
{{reflist|group=prep note|refs=
<ref name=from>In the sense of “from”, {{m|ro||de}} must contract into {{m|ro|în||in}} to form {{m|ro|din}}, into {{m|ro|între||between}} to form {{m|ro|dintre}}, and analogously into all adverbs derived from {{m|ro||în}}. The combination {{m|ro|de la}} is [[lexicalize|lexicalised]].</ref>
<ref name="by">When in a passive construction, {{m|ro||de}} can be followed by {{m|ro|către}} for clarification and to no change in meaning. This is typical of, but not restricted to, formal language.</ref><ref name="num">The {{m|ro||de}} that connects numerals to nouns may be omitted, but only in very formal, financial or legal language.</ref><ref name="due">Of the constructions using {{m|ro||de}} to mean “because of”, those that are not restricted to colloquial language are those referring to common bodily states: {{m|ro||de [[foame]]|because of hunger}}, {{m|ro||de [[sete]]|because of thirst}}, {{m|ro||de [[frig]]|because of cold}}, {{m|ro||de [[cald]]|because of heat}}, {{m|ro||de [[frică]]|for fear}}, {{m|ro||de [[somn]]|for lack of sleep}}, as well as with the name of any disease. Informally, an optional correlative sentence can be added using the connector {{m|ro|ce}}.

This meaning of {{m|ro||de}} is very similar to one of the senses of {{m|ro|de la}}. Most of the time, however, they are not interchangeable:

* {{m|ro||De}} can precede either a noun or an adjective, whereas {{m|ro|de la}} only precedes nouns.
* When preceding nouns, a cause introduced by {{m|ro||de}} generally represents a feeling that is experienced, while the cause introduced by {{m|ro|de la}} generally represents an event or an activity that worked to lead to a result in a manner understood by itself. Compare {{m|ro||de oboseală|out of exhaustion}} with {{m|ro||de la alergat|from all the running around|pos=which is understood to have led to exhaustion}}.
* The previous point can be disregarded if the determiner {{m|ro|atâta||so much}} is prepended to the noun, in which case {{m|ro||de}} is valid either way: {{m|ro||de atâta alergat|from so much running around}}.
* {{m|ro||De}} is more likely to have negative connotations than {{m|ro|de la}}.</ref><ref name="bout">In the sense of “about”, it can only be used after a verb, and not copulatively (“is about”) or after a noun (“a discussion about”). {{m|ro|despre|Despre}}, however, can be used in any of these situations.</ref>|style=lower-alpha}}

